translations.text:
To dare, fear, love, and hate oneself; gladly to be tormented by glory; to forget to forget one's troubles; amid flame to burn without catching fire;


to see oneself lonely among people and accompanied by loneliness; continually to die yet never end; to lose oneself to find a means of loss;


to be a rich Fugger of luckless hopes, spend all one's wealth on suffering, and conquer hard stone with wax: us


these are Love's effects in my laments. Let no one call him god—that is madness—for his torments belong more to an executioner.

That color of rose and lily, that delightful, sweet, honest gaze, that lovely white upright neck, and ruby mouth filled with pearls;


the alabaster hand that binds the one most set against love and banishes and estranges the freest, most tyrannical resolve from souls;


that rich and lovely spring, whose flowers of grace and beauty angry time cannot offend:


these cause me to live and die, and are the beginning, end, and relief of my care, my rest, and my happiness.

Since today grief pours night across the whole circle of pure light, and the sun, dimmed in dark shadow, gives tears to fire and voice to wind;


since death's black confinement reveals the grave with trembling, and the mountain obstructing the nearby sea's plain splits attentively;


your heart is hard stone, diamond, if so severe a death does not drown your face through your eyes.


Yet it is not stone, for if it were, pity at seeing loving God would have broken it among the other stones.
